{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786209992,"nonce":"W680iA9MCba4JGnEV12p5N/Zp7I=","title":"**What is wrong.** SPEC §G.7 still prints the ordering switch that was proven to","message":"wedge threads. In the SPEC's Go block (SPEC:2863-2871) the cases are ordered:\n\n```go\ncase d.Mode != domain.ModePostRoot \u0026\u0026 th.ProviderThreadID == \"\":\n    return river.JobSnooze(2 * time.Second)   // root has not landed yet\ncase d.ThreadSeq != th.LastSentSeq+1:\n    return river.JobSnooze(1 * time.Second)\ncase th.State == \"dead\":\n    return recoverThread(ctx, d, th)          // §G.7.3\n```\n\n\"root has not landed\" is tested *before* \"thread is dead\", which is exactly the\ncase the dead branch exists to rescue — the correctness audit traced it end to\nend: a root delivery that dies leaves the thread `opening` with a null\n`provider_thread_id`, so every later delivery matches case 1 and snoozes at 0.5 Hz\n\"forever, no attempt consumed, no dead-letter, no alert\". `e776716` inverted the\norder and added a bounded `MaxWait` escalation. The same SPEC block also still\nputs the provider call and the delivery update in one transaction (\"same tx:\nupdate delivery … + `channel_threads.last_sent_seq`\"), which `e776716` replaced\nwith three phases precisely because a commit failure re-posted the message.\n\n**Why it matters.** The SPEC is binding and is read literally by whoever\nimplements next; `docs/ORCHESTRATION.md:105-108` records this as awaiting\namendment. Two agents reading the same document currently get opposite answers,\nand the wrong answer is a silent 15-minute-to-forever outage on a Slack thread.\n\n**Where.** `docs/design/SPEC.md:2851-2890`, `docs/ORCHESTRATION.md:105-108`,\n`internal/platform/jobs/ordering/`, `internal/notification/service/dispatch.go`.\n\n**Done when.** §G.7 states the implemented order (dead first, then sequence, then\nroot), documents the bounded `MaxWait` escalation and the dead-letter outcome,\ndescribes the three-phase send with the claim durable before the network call, and\nlands with an ADR in the same commit as the SPEC requires.","files":null}]}